Consider a cell-free massive multiple-input multiple-output (CF-MMIMO) wireless system in which a large number L of geographically distributed access points (APs), each equipped with one or more antennas, jointly and coherently serve K single-antenna user equipments (UEs) over a shared time-frequency resource, with no cell boundaries and with user-centric clustering of APs. Let the uplink received signal at AP ℓ be y_ℓ = Σ_k g_{ℓk} x_k + n_ℓ, where g_{ℓk} ∈ ℂ^{N_ℓ} is the channel vector to UE k, x_k is its transmitted symbol, and n_ℓ is additive noise; APs are connected by fronthaul links of finite per-link capacity C_FH to one or more central processing units (CPUs) which perform joint coherent combining/precoding using channel state information (CSI) obtained via uplink pilots.

Three candidate physical infrastructures for realizing such a system at scale are:
(i) Radio Stripes — APs serially connected along a cable that carries power, synchronization, fronthaul data, and shared computation;
(ii) pCell-like centralized distributed antenna systems — distributed radio heads with centralized baseband processing at a single CPU;
(iii) RadioWeaves — large surfaces of integrated distributed antennas with embedded compute and storage.

Develop quantitative architectural models and provably scalable algorithms (with explicit per-AP fronthaul rate, per-CPU computational complexity, synchronization-error tolerance, and energy-per-bit scaling laws in L, K, and the system bandwidth B) that demonstrate, for each of (i)–(iii), whether the following practical limits can be simultaneously overcome:
(a) finite fronthaul capacity per link;
(b) real-time baseband data processing of the aggregate signal;
(c) phase and timing synchronization across APs adequate for coherent joint transmission/reception;
(d) support for UE mobility while maintaining CSI accuracy and user-centric clusters;
(e) initial access for unscheduled UEs in a cell-free setting;
(f) total energy efficiency (bits per Joule) scaling at least linearly in min(L,K);
(g) reliable powering and communication with energy-neutral backscatter devices.

Determine which of (i)–(iii) admits an implementation meeting (a)–(g), or prove fundamental impossibility/trade-off bounds among these constraints.

